# How this deck was made (W3C PROV-O): researched card by card from the sources below,
# each card checked against at least two of them, then in the review round below.
<#compilation>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-10T12:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:used <https://www.wikidata.org/> ,
              <https://archive.org/details/Fsi-KoreanBasicCourse-StudentText> ,
              <https://en.wiktionary.org/> ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith <#anton-wiklund> ;
    prov:wasInformedBy <#review-0> ,
                       <#review-1> ,
                       <#review-2> ,
                       <#review-3> ;
    rdfs:comment "Compiled by Anton Wiklund with the help of AI."@en ,
                 "Sammanställd av Anton Wiklund med hjälp av AI."@sv ,
                 "About this record: the comments on this compilation, its review round and its sources are condensed from the deck's dossier, the working record kept while the deck was compiled. The dossier's per-card evidence and Wikidata checks, its numbered queries and API calls (its Queries section), the scripts that ran them and its log of discrepancies are not part of this release; where the text refers to them, to the dossier or to findings listed below, it means that record. The builder is the script that built the deck from the dossier's card list and ran its Wikidata checks. The deck was compiled by an AI agent working for Anton Wiklund; the research, the writing of the cards and the four review rounds below (the compiling agent's own round 0 and three independent reviews, each done by a separate AI agent) were done by AI agents and by machines, and no person has reviewed the cards."@en ,
                 "The cards were checked by machine (live Wikidata, English Wiktionary's definitions and the app's SHACL and DCAT-AP validators)."@en ,
                 "Word list. A list of 125 words and phrases for a first Korean course was drawn up by the AI agent (not taken from a list) from the library editor's brief (numbers 0–20, the tens and 100; days; months; eight colours; eight family members; about twelve greetings and polite phrases; everyday nouns, adjectives and verbs), choosing the most basic, standard word for each concept and following the library's other basics decks where the languages allow. No frequency list, textbook word list or dictionary list was copied; the library's Korean noun, adjective and verb decks were read only for their conventions (dictionary forms in -다, Hangul only, card ids) and for the licences of their sources."@en ,
                 "Numbers. Korean has two sets of numbers. The cards use the Sino-Korean numbers (영, 일, 이, 삼 … 십, 이십 … 백), because only they run without a gap from 0 to 100 (the native numbers have no everyday words for 0 and 100), and because they are the ones used in the deck's own months (일월 … 십이월) as well as for dates, money, telephone numbers and minutes. Each card's note gives the native Korean number (하나, 둘, 셋 … 열, 스물 … 아흔) from Wikidata's numeral lexemes (L701012, L992182 …) and the Korean aliases of the number items (열하나 … 열아홉 for 11–19), which the FSI course confirms (its glossary marks each numeral Num Ch or Num K); 100 has no note. The notes on 1–4 also give the short form of the native number before a counter (한, 두, 세, 네), from the FSI course's Unit 4 grammar note 6 (\"When the numbers of Korean origin are used as modifiers, the final sounds of the first four are dropped, thus making hana 'one' han, tul 'two' tu, ses 'three' se, nes 'four' ne\"); the course gives no such form for 20 (스무), so its card has none."@en ,
                 "Wikidata lexemes. SPARQL query 1 fetched every Korean lexeme (language Q9176) whose lemma is one of 201 candidate forms (the cards, the native numbers and alternatives such as 엄마, 형, 년, 늙다), with its lexical category, senses, English and Korean glosses and sense items (P5137); query 1b added 48 more forms. They found lexemes for the numbers 1–10, the tens and 100 (none for 영 or 십일–십구, whose cards cite items only), the days, months, 네, 아니요, 안녕, 안녕하다, 괜찮다, 사람, 여자, 개, 고양이, 물, 학교, 우유, 길, 좋다, 없다, 있다, 하다, 가다, 먹다, 마시다, 보다 and 알다; the lexeme search (API calls 2) found none for 집, 책, 빵 or 사과, and most everyday Korean nouns, verbs and adjectives have no lexeme. The full JSON of the 82 lexemes used was fetched with API calls 3. The English sense glosses of 먹다 (L17), 마시다 (L154), 가다 (L204), 하다 (L226), 알다 (L148) and 보다 (L132, \"see; look at\") give those cards' English backs (the gloss without its explanation in brackets). Query 7 looked for lexemes in other languages whose senses translate (P5972) one of these Korean senses: it found five (English see, watch and notice, an Akkadian word for water and Czech škola), none in the deck's other languages but English, whose glosses agree with the cards."@en ,
                 "Wikidata items. The items linked from the senses and items found with the item search in Korean and English (API calls 6) and by Korean label (query 8) were fetched with API calls 5 (labels and aliases in ko, en, sv, de, es, fr and mul). They give the Korean word for numbers (as aliases), days, colours, family members and everyday things, and the English, Swedish, German, Spanish and French names of days, months, colours, family members and things, the mul digit label of each number, and the greetings thank you (Q19279214: 감사합니다, alias 고맙습니다), yes (Q6452715), no (Q1814990) and telephone hello (Q12835706: 여보세요)."@en ,
                 "The FSI course. The Internet Archive was searched for public-domain US Government Korean courses (call 9). It found the Foreign Service Institute's Korean Basic Course, Volume 1 (B. Nam Park, 1968), whose OCR text and scanned pages were downloaded (calls 10 and 11). The OCR text has the romanized Korean and the English but no Hangul, so the pages of the Korean-English Glossary (pp. 488–550) were fetched as images from the Internet Archive's page server and read directly; each glossary entry gives the word in FSI's transcription, in Hangul and in English with the unit where it occurs. The glossary is the content source for the Hangul and the meaning of the verbs and adjectives that have no lexeme (크다, 작다, 나쁘다, 많다, 비싸다, 춥다, 덥다, 오다, 자다, 사다, 읽다, 쓰다, 말하다, 살다, 좋아하다, and the meaning of 맛있다, for which it gives the phrase 맛이 있다), for the Hangul of the polite phrases it prints (the formal 안녕하십니까, 안녕히 가십시오 and 안녕히 계십시오, given in the notes, and 감사합니다, 고맙습니다, 미안합니다, 실례합니다, 괜찮습니다, 여보세요, 네, 아니요) and for 아이; it confirms most other words. The sense \"to have\" of 있다 is from the glossary (p. 503: Yələ kaci ka issɨmnita. 여러 가지가 있습니다. \"[We] have several kinds.\") and Unit 2's grammar notes (a nominal + i/ka + issɨmnita after a personal noun \"occurs to express that the personal noun has or possesses the nominal\": Na nɨn chæk i issɨmnita. 'I have a book.'). The OCR text was searched with grep for the notes on dialogues and the drills (call 12): Unit 1's notes on the greetings, Unit 4's Informal Polite Speech and its drill Annyəng-hase yo?, Unit 16's 공 in a telephone number and its Annyənghi kyese yo. Five fronts are not printed as they stand by any content source, and were obtained as follows. 안녕하세요: FSI prints only the formal 안녕하십니까 in Hangul and the -세요 form romanized (Unit 4 drill R, Annyəng-hase yo?); the Hangul is the authors' transcription. 안녕히 계세요: likewise from FSI's romanized Annyənghi kyese yo. (Unit 16) beside its Hangul 안녕히 계십시오. 안녕히 가세요: FSI has neither its Hangul nor its romanization; it is the authors' formation of FSI's 안녕히 가십시오 with the -세요 ending of the course's drills (kase yo), so only a verification source, English Wiktionary (\"goodbye\"), attests the form itself. 맛있다: FSI's glossary (p. 523) gives the phrase 맛이 있다 (mas i iss-ta, printed 맛이있다), and Wikidata has no lexeme; the one-word 맛있다 is the standard spelling, written by the authors and checked against English Wiktionary. 초록색: the authors' compound of the Korean label 초록 of green (Q3133) and 색 'colour' (the Korean label of Q1075; FSI sæk \"color\"), checked against English Wiktionary. These are facts of the language, so they do not affect the licence."@en ,
                 "Writing the cards. The front is the Korean word in Hangul only, with no romanization or hanja: nouns in their bare form, verbs and adjectives in the dictionary form in -다 (the form of the lexeme lemma and of FSI's glossary), days and months as words (월요일, 일월), colours as nouns in -색 (빨간색), and the greetings and polite phrases in the standard polite forms of today: the informal polite -세요 forms 안녕하세요, 안녕히 가세요 and 안녕히 계세요, and the formal polite 감사합니다, 고맙습니다, 미안합니다, 실례합니다 and 괜찮습니다. The back gives one text per language: the English, Swedish, German, Spanish and French meaning, or the digits for a number, tagged zxx (text in no language). Where a Wikidata item's label or alias is the everyday word, or a Korean lexeme's English sense gloss gives the meaning, the back uses it (317 of the 480 backs in the five languages are, wholly or in part, a Wikidata label, alias or gloss); the backs keep the deck's typographic apostrophe (aujourd’hui) and capitalize German colour nouns (Schwarz) where the label differs only in that; otherwise the meaning is the authors' own rendering, written with the help of AI, from the English gloss of the lexeme or of FSI's glossary, and checked in English Wiktionary. A Korean verb or adjective is rendered as a verb in every language (to be big, att vara stor, groß sein, ser grande, être grand), since Korean adjectives are verbs. Where one word in another language does not cover the Korean, two are given (집: house, home; 길: road, way; 밥: cooked rice, meal), and the greetings for leaving say who is leaving. Swedish writes days and months in lower case. Every text is tagged with its language: the front ko, the backs and notes en, sv, de, es and fr, digits zxx."@en ,
                 "Card ids are the letter-by-letter Revised Romanization of the Hangul, syllable by syllable and without hyphens or sound changes (사람 saram, 좋다 jotda, 좋아하다 jotahada, 실례합니다 silryehapnida, 안녕하세요 annyeonghaseyo, 감사합니다 gamsahapnida), the rule of the library's Korean decks; they are addresses, not shown as text."@en ,
                 "Notes. A short note, in all five languages, was added where it helps a beginner and a content source confirms it: the native Korean number on every number from 1 to 90; 공 for zero (FSI: kong \"zero\", and 3-6503 read sam e yuk o kong sam); other words spelt like 일 (work, and day in dates), 이 (this, tooth) and 팔 (arm), from FSI's glossary and the lexemes L744807, L131 and L328; the digit form of each month (1월 …, the Korean labels of the month items) and why 유월 and 시월 drop a letter (the forms are Wikidata aliases and lexeme lemmas; the explanation is the authors'); other names for six colours (빨강, 파랑, 초록, 녹색, 노랑, 검정, 까만색, 하얀색: Wikidata labels and aliases and FSI); 엄마 and 아빠 (Wikidata aliases, FSI); 부인 for someone else's wife (FSI: puin \"lady, your wife\"); the more formal forms of the greetings and their literal sense, “go in peace”, “stay in peace” (FSI: annyəng \"peace\", annyənghi \"peacefully\", kasipsiyo, kyesipsiyo); the uses of 안녕 (meeting and parting: the lexeme's gloss; that it is informal, among friends, is the authors' own knowledge, confirmed only by English Wiktionary), 실례합니다, 여보세요, 네 and 아니요 (FSI's notes on dialogues); 미안합니다 also acknowledging that someone has gone to trouble for you (the authors' modern wording of FSI's Unit 1 note 21, which says it also expresses \"thanks immediately upon receiving something\", a 1968 usage stated too broadly for today); 괜찮습니다 as the answer to an apology (FSI note 28) and as a polite \"no, thank you\" (the authors' addition, within FSI's gloss \"That's OK\"); 감사합니다 and 고맙습니다 interchangeable (FSI Unit 2 note 8-18, \"freely interchangeable\"), with the authors' addition that 감사합니다 is a little more formal; the short forms 한, 두, 세, 네 before a counter (FSI Unit 4 grammar note 6); 사과 also meaning apology (item Q13197791); 어린이 beside 아이 (the label of Q7569); 키가 크다, 나이가 많다, 차다 beside 춥다 and 뜨겁다 beside 덥다 (FSI); the other senses of 쓰다 (FSI); and 춥다 and 덥다 of the weather or of how one feels (FSI chup-ta \"is cold\", təp-ta \"is hot\", with 차다 and 뜨겁다 for things). Notes are the authors' own wording."@en ,
                 "Cross-checking. Every Korean front, and every word of the Swedish, German, Spanish and French backs, was looked up in English Wiktionary's definitions (calls 13, the REST API, the section of that language), and the card's English meaning looked for in the definitions. All 125 fronts have a Korean entry (a phrase in -합니다 or -습니다 under its dictionary form), and 124 have the card's meaning in a definition; the one that has not, 실례합니다, is defined by its literal sense, \"to be rude\", which the note gives. 452 of the 452 words of the other backs have an entry in their language, 433 with the English meaning; the other 19 were read one by one by the agent and agree (inflected forms such as kallt and varmt, near-synonyms such as zahlreich and numeroso, phrases such as ingen fara looked up by one word, and a Wiktionary page that did not render). Wiktionary also confirms that 아니오, the Korean label of Wikidata's no (Q1814990), is a misspelling of 아니요."@en ,
                 "Dictionaries not used. The library's Korean decks took their meanings from the Basic Korean Dictionary (krdict, CC BY-SA 2.0 KR) and their ranking from the National Institute of Korean Language's frequency survey (Korea Open Government Licence type 1, attribution). Neither licence allows content in a CC0 deck, so neither was consulted, nor the Standard Korean Language Dictionary; Korean Wiktionary and Folkets lexikon were not consulted either. English Wiktionary, whose licence allows automated use, verified the cards. The FSI course's Volume 2 was downloaded but not used."@en ,
                 "Machine checks. The dossier's Wikidata checks (562 checks on 105 cards, against 96 items and 82 lexemes: the Korean label or alias of each linked item that matches the front, or for a month the digit label in its note; the English, Swedish, German, Spanish and French labels or aliases that match the back, wholly or in part; the mul digit label of each number; the Korean aliases and native numeral lexemes behind the number notes; the labels and lemmas behind the other notes; the head of the lexeme's English sense gloss behind six English backs; and the lemma of every cited lexeme; where Wikidata's text differs from the card's only in case or apostrophe style, as Schwarz beside the label schwarz and the card's aujourd’hui, with a typographic apostrophe, beside the label aujourd'hui, the check quotes Wikidata's text exactly) are re-run against live Wikidata by the builder on every build. Where a label does not match the card, no check was added and the difference is listed in the dossier's discrepancies (for example the item labels 남성, 여성, 어린이, 명칭 and 도로 beside the aliases 남자, 여자, 이름 and 길; 초록 for 초록색; the Swedish labels förälders far and förälders mor). The 20 cards that cite no Wikidata entity (16 verbs and adjectives and four polite phrases) rest on the FSI course. The built deck was validated with the library checker (the app's SHACL shapes, DCAT-AP and the library's rules)."@en ,
                 "Excluded: brother and sister, since Korean names them by age and by the speaker's sex (형, 오빠, 누나, 언니, 동생; FSI glosses them so), so husband and wife take their places; 책상 (FSI: table, desk) and 싸다 (cheap, but also to wrap), to keep one sense per card; 처음 뵙겠습니다, 죄송합니다 and the copula 이다, for which no CC0 or public-domain source gives the modern form or which are grammar rather than vocabulary; old and new (늙다 for people, 오래되다 for things; 새 is a determiner) and warm, which have no single basic word; day, night, week and year (날, 밤, 주, 해, 년), which are bound nouns or spelt like other words (밤 also chestnut, 해 also sun); the native numbers as cards (they are in the notes); and the colour adjectives 빨갛다, 파랗다, 노랗다, 까맣다 and 하얗다, since the colours are nouns in -색 on the cards."@en ,
                 "Selection: 125 cards: the cardinal numbers 0–20, the tens 30–90 and 100, in the Sino-Korean system (29); the seven days of the week; the twelve months; eight colours (빨간색, 파란색, 초록색, 노란색, 검은색, 흰색, 회색, 갈색); eight family members (어머니, 아버지, 아들, 딸, 할아버지, 할머니, 남편, 아내); twelve greetings and polite phrases (안녕하세요, 안녕, 안녕히 가세요, 안녕히 계세요, 감사합니다, 고맙습니다, 미안합니다, 실례합니다, 괜찮습니다, 여보세요, 네, 아니요); 24 everyday nouns (사람, 남자, 여자, 아이, 친구, 이름, 집, 학교, 물, 밥, 빵, 우유, 사과, 책, 의자, 문, 창문, 개, 고양이, 길, 돈, 손, 오늘, 내일); nine adjectives (크다, 작다, 좋다, 나쁘다, 많다, 비싸다, 춥다, 덥다, 맛있다); 있다 and 없다 (to be there, to exist, to have, and their negation; 없다 is an adjective in Korean grammar and 있다 both a verb and an adjective, so they are counted apart); and 14 verbs (하다, 가다, 오다, 먹다, 마시다, 보다, 자다, 사다, 읽다, 쓰다, 말하다, 살다, 알다, 좋아하다). The list was chosen by the deck's authors for a first course, not copied from any frequency list, textbook or dictionary. The deck is studied front to back only: some backs are the same in a language (감사합니다 and 고맙습니다 are both thank you), so the reverse direction would be ambiguous."@en ,
                 "Licensing: The deck is CC0 1.0. Its content comes from two sources whose terms allow any reuse: Wikidata's lexemes and items (CC0 1.0: Korean lemmas, labels and aliases, glosses, and the English, Swedish, German, Spanish and French labels) and the Foreign Service Institute's Korean Basic Course, Volume 1 (1968), a work of the United States Government with no copyright protection under 17 U.S.C. § 105 (the Hangul and meaning of the verbs, adjectives and polite phrases without a Wikidata entry, and the facts behind most notes). Section 105 applies in the United States; what the deck takes from the course is the Korean form and English meaning of single words and standard phrases and such facts of usage, which are not protectable expression anywhere, so the deck's CC0 dedication does not depend on § 105 applying outside the US. The selection of words is the authors' own. The meanings not taken from Wikidata are the authors' own renderings, written with the help of AI. Fourteen of their English backs are word for word in one of English Wiktionary's definitions of the Korean word (person, to be big, to be small, to be good, to be bad, to be hot, to come, to sleep, to buy, to read, to write, to live, to like, I’m sorry) and several others are close (goodbye, hello on the telephone, cooked rice, to be many, to be expensive, to be cold, to be delicious, to speak, to be somewhere, to exist, to have); each of them follows the public-domain gloss of the FSI course (salam \"person\", khɨ-ta \"is big\", cak-ta \"is small\", coh-ta \"is good\", nappɨta \"is bad\", təp-ta \"is hot\", o-ta \"comes\", ca-ta \"sleeps\", sa-ta \"buys\", ilk-ta \"reads\", ssɨ-ta \"writes\", sa(l)-ta \"lives\", cohaha-ta \"likes\", Mianhamnita \"I'm sorry\"; for the close ones, among others, ka-ta \"goes\" with annyənghi \"peacefully\", Yəpose yo \"Hello there!\", pap \"rice (cooked), meal\", iss-ta \"exists, is\" and \"have\"), rendered in the deck's to-form; #bap and #manda are also close to the library's Korean noun and adjective decks (CC BY-SA), whose meanings were not used, and follow FSI too (pap \"rice (cooked), meal\", manh-ta \"is plenty, are many\"). They are the same short stock glosses that English Wiktionary gives, but single words and fixed greetings such as \"thank you\" or \"hej då\" are facts of usage, not protectable expression, so this does not carry Wiktionary's licence into the deck; no definition, example or list was taken from it. English Wiktionary (CC BY-SA 4.0) was used for verification only. So no source requires attribution or share-alike for the deck, although all are credited in its provenance. Quotations from English Wiktionary in the dossier are © Wiktionary contributors, CC BY-SA 4.0, and are not covered by the deck's CC0 dedication; quotations from the FSI course are in the public domain; Wikidata data is CC0."@en .

<#review-0>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-10T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Sources, licences and machine checks"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 125 cards: every Korean form, meaning in the five back languages and note against the content sources (Wikidata, the FSI course) and English Wiktionary; the licence evidence of the three sources; a re-run of the builder's Wikidata checks against live Wikidata on 10 October 2026; the library checker. Done by the AI agent that compiled the deck and by machines; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: Every card has evidence from a content source: 105 cards cite Wikidata and the other 20 the FSI course, which also confirms most of the 105. English Wiktionary has every front and every word of the other backs. The re-run of the Wikidata checks covered every card that cites Wikidata: 562 checks on 105 cards, against 96 items and 82 lexemes; they all pass. The library checker reports the deck valid. Discrepancies between the sources (Wikidata's label 아니오 for no, FSI's older spelling 설흔 for 서른, FSI's formal greetings beside today's -세요 forms, item labels that are not the everyday word) and how they were resolved are listed in the dossier."@en .

<#review-1>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-10T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Factual accuracy"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 125 cards (every front, every back in the five languages and every note) against live Wikidata (every cited item and lexeme fetched again, every Wikidata check in the dossier re-run, and a SPARQL query for Korean lexemes of the words the record says have none), the FSI course's Volume 1 (the OCR text of the notes cited and the scanned glossary pages) and the library's other basics decks. Done by an AI agent working independently of the compiling agent; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: No errors: the numbers, days, months, colours, family words and nouns and their meanings were found correct, every Wikidata check passed, and the words said to have no Korean lexeme have none. Two warnings, both applied: the evidence for 있다 “to have” cited the wrong page and a different expression (가져 있다, “to be holding”), and now cites FSI p. 503 (여러 가지가 있습니다, “[We] have several kinds”) and Unit 2's grammar note on possession (“I have a book”); for 맛있다 the record now says that FSI gives the phrase 맛이 있다 and that the one-word spelling is the authors', checked against English Wiktionary. Suggestions applied: the Swedish, German, Spanish and French backs of 춥다 and 덥다 now cover feeling cold or hot as well as the weather (att frysa, frieren, tener frío, avoir froid and their counterparts), and the Swedish and German notes were clarified; the note on 미안합니다, which reflected FSI's 1968 usage, was softened; the note on 괜찮습니다 adds the polite “no, thank you”; the notes on 감사합니다 and 고맙습니다 add, as the authors' own addition, that 감사합니다 is a little more formal; the descriptions and the selection no longer count 있다 and 없다 among the verbs (없다 is an adjective in Korean grammar) and name them apart; the German notes on the two goodbyes use the polite Sie; the English backs of 먹다, 마시다, 가다, 하다, 알다 and 보다 are credited to their lexemes' Wikidata glosses, which the builder now checks; the Wikidata checks quote the label exactly where it differs from the card only in apostrophe style (aujourd'hui) or case; and the notes on 1–4 give the forms 한, 두, 세, 네 before a counter, from FSI's Unit 4 grammar note 6. Declined: the form 스무 for 20, since no CC0 or public-domain source found gives it (FSI says the other numbers do not change); and crediting the back of 안녕 to the gloss of L747234, which describes the greeting (“friendly greeting both in meeting and in parting”) rather than rendering it; the note's meeting-and-parting fact is credited to that gloss."@en .

<#review-2>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-10T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Language, translation and language tags"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 125 cards, line by line (the Korean front, the five backs and the five notes), and the deck's titles, descriptions and keywords in all six languages, the language tags and the study direction, compared with the library's basics decks and Korean decks, the FSI course's notes as quoted in the dossier and live Wikidata. Done by an AI agent working independently of the compiling agent; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: No errors: the Korean spelling and spacing, the tags (ko, zxx and one tag per back and note), the ids and the counts were found correct. Four warnings, all applied: the descriptions in all six languages now say that the polite phrases are in their standard polite form, with the informal 안녕, rather than that all greetings are; the backs of 많다 now read viel sein, zahlreich sein; ser numeroso, haber mucho; être nombreux, être en grande quantité (a verb phrase in the être pattern of the other French backs, in place of the reviewer's “(il y en a) beaucoup”); the note on 미안합니다 was softened, as in review 1; and the Swedish and German notes on 춥다 and 덥다 were rewritten (när man fryser, när man själv är varm, wenn einem kalt ist, wenn einem heiß ist). Suggestions applied: the Swedish backs att vara kallt (om vädret), att frysa and att vara varmt (om vädret), att vara varm; 없다 as not to be (somewhere), not to exist; not to have, with the same change in Swedish, Spanish and French; the German notes on the goodbyes in the polite Sie; the note on 괜찮습니다 now gives the answer to an apology and the polite “no, thank you”, and no longer the answer to thanks; the Spanish back of 여보세요 aló, diga (al teléfono), since aló alone is Latin American; slutkonsonant in the Swedish notes on 유월 and 시월; the note on 감사합니다 being a little more formal; je suis désolé(e); marron, brun for brown in French (the alias and the label of Q47071); the English notes in double quotation marks, as the template decks write them; the Korean description's 미국 국무부 외교연수원 and 위키낱말사전에서 확인했습니다; and 일 meaning “day” in dates (3일). Declined: none."@en .

<#review-3>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-10T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Licensing, attribution and documentation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 125 cards against the dossier, the working files and live sources: the licence evidence of the three sources (Wikidata:Licensing, the English Wiktionary footer, the Internet Archive metadata, the FSI title page and preface, 17 U.S.C. § 105), a live re-run of the Wikidata checks and of the claims that words have no Korean lexeme, every FSI-only gloss and greeting form in the OCR text, the Wiktionary cross-check counts, the AI-written English backs against English Wiktionary and the library's CC BY-SA Korean decks, the card ids against the library's Korean decks, the metadata against the template decks, and the attribution and review wording. Done by an AI agent working independently of the compiling agent; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: The CC0 decision, the three source records, the attribution comments and the metadata were found correct, and nothing in the record claims that a person reviewed the deck. Four warnings, all applied: the ids of 좋아하다 and 실례합니다 broke the library's letter-by-letter rule and are now #jotahada and #silryehapnida (as korean-verbs has jotahada, nolrada and dalrida); the record called FSI or Wikidata the source of five fronts that neither prints as they stand, and now says how 안녕하세요, 안녕히 가세요, 안녕히 계세요, 맛있다 and 초록색 were obtained, and that FSI's glossary gives the formal greetings; and “by hand” was replaced by the agent's work, since no person took part. Suggestions applied: the AI-written English backs that match English Wiktionary are named in its source record and in the licensing paragraph, with the FSI glosses they follow (the backs of 보다, 먹다 and four other verbs are now credited to Wikidata's lexeme glosses instead, see review 1); the FSI record's creator names its editor, Augustus A. Koski; the lexeme paragraph says the lexemes cover 1–10, the tens and 100, not 0 or 11–19; the check on 오늘 quotes Wikidata's aujourd'hui exactly; the dossier's list of Wiktionary misses is now generated from the cross-check results (it had left out sv mat and counted de da once); and the register of 안녕 is given as the authors' knowledge, confirmed only by English Wiktionary. Declined: none."@en .

<https://www.wikidata.org/>
    dcterms:title "Wikidata (Korean lexemes and items)" ;
    dcterms:creator "Wikidata contributors (Wikimedia Foundation)" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/> ;
    rdfs:comment "Content: Korean lemmas, labels and aliases, glosses, and the labels in English, Swedish, German, Spanish and French and the digit labels. https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Wikidata:Licensing — \"All structured data (i.e. the main, Property, Lexeme, and EntitySchema namespaces) is released into the public domain under Creative Commons Zero.\""@en .

<https://archive.org/details/Fsi-KoreanBasicCourse-StudentText>
    dcterms:title "Korean Basic Course, Volume 1 (Foreign Service Institute, 1968), as scanned on the Internet Archive" ;
    dcterms:creator "B. Nam Park; edited by Augustus A. Koski; Foreign Service Institute, U.S. Department of State" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/> ;
    rdfs:comment "Content: the Hangul and English meaning of verbs, adjectives and polite phrases, and the facts behind most notes, from the Korean-English Glossary (pp. 488–550, read in the scanned pages) and the notes on dialogues. A work of the United States Government: the title page reads \"B. Nam Park … Foreign Service Institute, Washington, D.C. 1968\" and \"For sale by the Superintendent of Documents, U.S. Government Printing Office\", and the preface says \"B. Nam Park, supervisor of Korean language instruction at FSI, is the principal author of the text\" and that the course was prepared with FSI's staff and the financial assistance of the U.S. Office of Education. 17 U.S.C. § 105 (https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/17/105): \"Copyright protection under this title is not available for any work of the United States Government\". The Internet Archive item's metadata (https://archive.org/metadata/Fsi-KoreanBasicCourse-StudentText) gives licenseurl \"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/publicdomain/\". Recorded here with the Public Domain Mark: free of known copyright."@en .

<https://en.wiktionary.org/>
    dcterms:title "English Wiktionary (Korean, Swedish, German, Spanish and French entries)" ;
    dcterms:creator "Wiktionary contributors" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/> ;
    rdfs:comment "Verification only; no content was taken from it. Fourteen of the authors' English backs are word for word in one of its definitions of the Korean word (#saram, #keuda, #jakda, #jotda, #nappeuda, #deopda, #oda, #jada, #sada, #ikda, #sseuda, #salda, #jotahada, #mianhapnida) and ten are close (#annyeonghigaseyo, #annyeonghigyeseyo, #yeoboseyo, #bap, #manda, #bissada, #chupda, #matitda, #malhada, #itda); all follow the FSI course's public-domain glosses and are stock glosses, not taken from Wiktionary. https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/사람 — page footer: \"Definitions and other text are available under the Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike License; additional terms may apply.\", linking to https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/."@en .

